# SYNOPSIS

```usage
dosfs [-r] [-u user]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`dosfs` serves a FAT12, FAT16 or FAT32 volume as 9Px: the EFI system partition, a USB stick, an SD card.
It is started by svcd(8), whose manifest (see svc(6)) connects it to the disk (`connect=`) and names its post (`post=`); it takes the one connector named `srv:<name>` as the disk and opens a block session on it.
It reads the boot sector, prints what it found, and serves the volume.
The type is decided by the cluster count, as Microsoft's specification has it.

It reads and writes: files and directories are made, written, truncated, removed, and renamed with the posix extension's `Trenameat`, and changed with `Tsetattr`.
Writes go to the disk as they are made, in an order that leaves a volume a crash cuts short with lost clusters at worst, never one file's clusters in another.
Both FATs are written.
`Tfsync`, and the clunk of a fid opened for writing, flush the disk and FAT32's FSInfo.

The tree is attached with an empty aname; any other is refused.

: `-r`
  Serve the volume read-only.
  A disk the block session reports read-only is served read-only without it.
: `-u` <user>
  The owner and group every file is shown with; `none` without it.

## Names

A long name, UTF-16 on the disk, is served as UTF-8; an entry with none is served by its 8.3 name, lower-cased where Windows NT's case flags say.
A lookup ignores ASCII case and matches an entry's long name or its 8.3 alias.
A new name that is a plain 8.3 name in one case is written as one, with NT's flags and no long name; any other gets a long name and a unique alias in Windows' manner, `BASIS~N.EXT`.
A name holding a control character, one of `"*/:<>?\|`, or ending in a dot or space is refused.
Deleted entries, the volume label, `.` and `..` are not listed; hidden and system files are.

## Owners and permissions

FAT has neither, so `dosfs` makes them up: every file is <user>'s, `0644`, and every directory `0755`.
An entry marked read-only is `0444` (a directory `0555`), and so is everything with `-r`.
Changing the mode turns the read-only mark on when the owner's write bit is off and off when it is on; the other bits are not kept.
Changing the owner or group is refused.
A read-only entry cannot be opened for writing.
Nothing else is checked: whoever can reach the post can change any file.

Only files and directories can be made: a create asking for any other kind, such as a symbolic link, is refused.

## Nodes

A file's node is its entry's place: the first cluster of the directory it is in and the index of its entry there.
A rename moves the entry, so the old node is remembered as moved, for fids that still hold it; the last 64 renames are remembered.
A file's times are stamped from the wall clock, the qid's version is its modification time in seconds, and its access time is FAT's, which is a day.

# EXAMPLES

The installed system's manifest for the ESP, from `boot/svc/system.ndb`:

```ndb
service=sys-dosfs program=/boot/bin/dosfs post=esp console when=vx.system
connect=disk0.esp
arg=-u
arg=adm
```

what it prints:

```text
dosfs: /srv/disk0.esp: FAT32 "VECTRA", 129008 clusters of 512 bytes
```

and, with `-r` on a test's FAT32 disk:

```text
dosfs: /srv/disk1: FAT32 "VECTRAFAT", 130040 clusters of 1024 bytes, read-only
```

# FILES

: `/srv/<post>`
  Its post, as the manifest's `post=` names it: `/srv/esp` on an installed system.

