# DIAGNOSTICS

At start it prints the disk, the FAT type, the volume's label if it has one, its clusters and their size, and `read-only` when it is so.
A failure to start prints `dosfs: FAILED:` and what failed, with the error when there is one, and exits with what failed:

: `no connector to a disk (connect=)`
  The manifest connects it to no disk.
: `no listen channel (post=)`
  The manifest posts nothing.
: `cannot open a session on the disk`
  The block session was refused.
: `no FAT volume on the disk`
  The boot sector is not FAT's.
: `the volume's sectors are smaller than the disk's`
  The volume's sector size is not a multiple of the disk's.

An argument it does not know prints the usage message, after `dosfs: FAILED:`, and exits with it.
It exits `cannot serve` if serving fails.
A write to a full volume fails with `file system full`, keeping what was written.

# BUGS

A short name's bytes past ASCII are in a DOS code page the volume does not name, and are served as U+FFFD; lookups fold ASCII case only.

Times are FAT's local time taken as UTC, to two seconds, and access times to the day.

A file removed while it is open is gone at once, and a fid that held a renamed entry finds it only while the rename is among the last 64; a node is a place, so a fid to a removed entry may later find a new entry made there.

Finding a node's entry or parent reads its directory from its start, so directories of thousands of entries are slow.
A FAT chain that loops is bounded by the file's size and the cluster count, not detected.

Owners are the `-u` user's alone, which the posix userland shows as a number.

The dirty bit is never set, and the backup boot sector and FSInfo's copy are not kept up to date.
Writes are synchronous, sector by sector, so large writes are slow.

In QEMU only FAT16 is written; FAT12 and FAT32 writing are tested on the host only, as is `Tsetattr`'s size, since sbase has no `truncate`.
